        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">A puddle is a deadline, not a blemish.</strong> Standing water strips binder from the aggregate, saturates and weakens the base, and in freezing climates breaks the surface apart from underneath. The pothole in three years will be exactly where the water sits now.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Measure the depression with a straightedge, not the puddle. Under half an inch takes a feathered skim patch; half an inch to an inch and a half takes cold patch or hot mix properly compacted; deeper than that means the base has failed and needs cutting out and rebuilding.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Never fill a low spot with sealer, never under-compact a patch, and before any of it, check whether a downspout is feeding the problem. If the water sits on a flat rather than in a dip, no patch will help — that is a slope problem and it needs a drain or an overlay.
          </p>
